rexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412629 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "65f2c34a-43ea-5ba9-8212-652326193c1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412629 #edb/ref 1074 "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀n\\in N,\\,11n-{n}^{2}-28\\le 0.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀n\\in N,\\,11n-{n}^{2}-28\\le 0)\\,≡\\,∃n\\in N,\\,11n-{n}^{2}-28>0\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists a natural number $n$ such that \n\n$$\n11n-{n}^{2}-28>0.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=5$ suffices:\n\nBy substituting $n=5$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11n-{n}^{2}-28 & =11(5)-(5)^{2}-28 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $n=5$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412630 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412630 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412630 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412630 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412630 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412630 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412630 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412630 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412630 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412630 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221701" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412630 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412630 #edb/ref 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$5n+2$ is odd for any $n\\in Z.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}} \n$5n+2$ is {{field-4}}.\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5n+2 & =5({{field-6}})+2 \\\\  & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is {{field-8}}. \n\nSo, $n=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412630 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7927d3cc-3f03-52c6-bf05-98c129843c5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412630 #edb/ref 1074 "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀n\\in Z,\\,5n+2\\text{ is odd}.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀n\\in Z,\\,5n+2\\text{ is odd})\\,≡\\,∃n\\in Z,\\,5n+2\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that $5n+2$ is even.\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=0$ suffices:\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=0$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5n+2 & =5(0)+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is even. \n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $n=0$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412631 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412631 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412631 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412631 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412631 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412631 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412631 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412631 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412631 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412631 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221707" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412631 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412631 #edb/ref 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$6∣{n}^{2}+2$ for any $n\\in Z.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n6{{field-4}}{n}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2 & =({{field-6}})^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is {{field-8}} by $6.$ \n\nSo, $n=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412631 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c18b42a5-90d9-54a8-87df-2489d5f7b5e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412631 #edb/ref 1074 "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀n\\in Z,\\,6∣{n}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀n\\in Z,\\,6∣{n}^{2}+2)\\,≡\\,∃n\\in Z,\\,6∤{n}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that \n\n$$\n6∤{n}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=0$ suffices:\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=0$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2 & =(0)^{2}+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $6.$ \n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $n=0$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412632 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221834" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412632 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412632 #edb/ref 1071 "Disprove the following statement:\n\n${x}^{2}+5x+6\\ge 0$ for any $x\\in R.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+5x+6{{field-4}}0.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+6 & ={({{field-6}})}^{2}+5({{field-7}})+6 \\\\  & ={{field-8}} \\\\  & {{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=$ {{field-10}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412632 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbf3d356-bb6f-5d45-9a65-7f6a6ff861ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412632 #edb/ref 1074 "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in R,\\,{x}^{2}+5x+6\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀x\\in R,\\,{x}^{2}+5x+6\\ge 0)\\,≡\\,∃x\\in R,\\,{x}^{2}+5x+6<0\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists a real number $x$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+5x+6<0.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $x=-2.5$ suffices:\n\nBy substituting $x=-2.5$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+6 & =(-2.5)^{2}+5(-2.5)+6 \\\\  & =-0.25 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $x=-2.5$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
